WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.968
Uh, uh, uh, uh, uh, uh, uh, uh.

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:07.440
[LIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.880
We're really excited
to have one of the best

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.400
saxophonists in the world.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.520
He's coming to MIT
because he knows

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.320
of the talent of these
students, I mean,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.300
the Festival Jazz
Ensemble is just one

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.875
of the best musical
groups in Boston.

00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:30.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.730
There's still close to
a million Puerto Ricans

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.070
without electricity.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.180
My grandmother just received
electricity like a week ago.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.550
It's been five months.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.750
With this concert, the
Jazz Ensembles initiative

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.840
was to be able to
collect all this money

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.180
and donate it to the
Puerto Rico Recovery

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.035
Fund, which is a long-term
vision for the island.

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:07.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:11.570
There's still a lot of
chaos, unfortunately,

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.930
going on in the island.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.660
So we're just trying to
really create awareness.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.870
Anything you can do to help
is greatly needed and greatly

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.330
appreciated by the
folks down there.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.770
I have a lot of friends from
artists to most small business

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.950
owners who decided to stay
there and do what they could

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.440
to build that country back up.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.600
So I wrote this piece
for them, thinking

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.819
about the resiliency
and the courage of a lot

00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:39.110
of these people in Puerto Rico.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.000
A lot of the people
are trying to help out

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.346
the situation there.

00:01:42.346 --> 00:01:45.818
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:00.202 --> 00:02:03.870
How do we create more
resilient infrastructures,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.680
electrical grids,
housing, water systems--

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.080
try to find ways of
decentralizing the whole thing,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.690
so that if like one
tower falls, some people

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.210
would be able to sustain their
own micro-grid solar panels

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.240
and stuff like that.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.656
So I think that's
where MIT really

00:02:17.656 --> 00:02:20.730
comes in, right, is to try to
bring all the innovation that

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.550
are in these labs to
come to Puerto Rico--

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.107
make a difference
in people's lives.

00:02:25.107 --> 00:02:28.446
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.730
And we're looking for
the future to have

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.490
a bit of like a setup
externship, where you could

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.040
have students work
on actual projects

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.710
that could benefit the
Puerto Rican community

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.450
and society on the island--

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.200
brainstorming ideas, and a bit
of consulting and designing.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.455
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.710
I hope that young
people don't take this

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.230
as a kind of a
defeat, but more so

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.870
as an opportunity
to grow and be part

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.120
of the Puerto Rico of tomorrow.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.080
If the young people give up, the
rest is just going to collapse.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.427
We can't let our country die.

00:03:09.427 --> 00:03:10.421
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:10.421 --> 00:03:13.900
[STOMPING]

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:17.873
(IN UNISON) Hey!

00:03:17.873 --> 00:03:18.373
[CHEERING]

00:03:18.373 --> 00:03:21.852
[APPLAUSE]

